From e90fc13e8dfb4952ff3939c212aa9412ac0af778 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <ms178@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 17:08:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/8] aarch64: f128 sign-bit intrinsics + full-precision copy
 tracking
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three fixes from the glibc_ld_copysign red-team:

1. LDCopysign/LDFabs/F128Copysign/F128Fabs (and F128Neg for parity with
   x86) had NO AArch64 lowering — they hit the silent x86-only no-op and
   returned whatever was live in q0. Implement as pure GPR bit ops on
   the slot-homed 16 bytes: AArch64 long double is binary128, so the
   sign bit is bit 63 of the HIGH qword (mask materialized as
   movz x10,#0x8000,lsl#48; bic/and/eor; no soft-float call, NaN payload
   preserved). Dest is marked track_f128_self so later loads take the
   16-byte path.

2. Copy dropped f128 source tracking. The f128 operand loaders pick
   between a 16-byte slot load and the lossy extend(f64(low qword))
   fallback based on f128_load_sources; a Copy through an untracked
   temporary silently downgraded every later compare/store of the copy
   (observed at -O2: cs(-2.0L,3.0L)==2.0L compared the low qword
   reinterpreted as a double). Propagate the entry in the shared default
   emit_copy_value (RISC-V + any future backend) and in the AArch64
   override. -O0/-O1 passed because no Copy existed there.

3. emit_call_store_f128_result spilt q1 below sp and libcall'd
   __trunctfdf2 on it after every f128-returning call — dead code: q1 is
   caller-clobbered by the call (it held the second argument, not the
   result), and the x0 'approximation' was overwritten before any
   consumer. One libcall + two memory round-trips saved per f128 call.

glibc_ld_copysign passes at -O0/-O1/-O2/-O3; ARM regression 369/36/75;
lib tests 1270/0/6.
