From f66a59057c3f9f29f1a816b5f39c5ccb27cc9444 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <ms178@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 17:41:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] aarch64: honor AAPCS64 for _Float128 (IrType::U128
 carrier)

_Float128 lowers to IrType::U128 with CallInfo.is_f128_sse /
IrParam.is_f128_sse / ret_is_f128_sse set on both call sides, but the
ARM backend ignored every one of those flags:

  * classify_args_core emitted the x86-only CoreArgClass::F128SseReg
    (one XMM register) for 16-byte _Float128 scalars unconditionally.
    ARM consumed neither that class nor ret_is_f128_sse, so parameters
    were staged into GP register pairs and returns took the i128
    x0:x1 path - every _Float128 argument/return was garbage.

  * classify_args_core now classifies per target: AAPCS64
    (config.f128_in_fp_regs) assigns Q registers via F128FpReg with
    F128Stack overflow, reusing the long-double machinery end to end;
    x86-64 keeps F128SseReg (the branch is gated on f128_in_fp_regs,
    which only ARM sets - x86/i686/riscv classification is untouched;
    x86 suite and hand-checked passthru asm are byte-identical).

  * the ARM return path now checks func_ret_is_f128_sse and loads the
    16-byte carrier into q0 (slot-direct fast path; over-aligned and
    register-resident fallbacks); the call-result store intercepts
    call_ret_is_f128_sse and emits a single `str q0` instead of
    routing U128 through the x0:x1 i128 pair.

  * caller staging of carrier arguments no longer falls back to
    __extenddftf2 for values that are already full 16-byte carriers:
    F128Reg staging loads the source slot directly when the argument
    type is U128, and I128 constants stage their bits bit-exactly
    instead of integer-converting the binary128 pattern through
    to_f64() (1.5F128 previously materialized as ~8.5e37).

ARM regression: 371 passed / 34 failed / 75 skipped (was 369/36/75,
no regressions); f1/f2/f3 oracle parity ident=1.50 add3=4.50
mix=10.6875. Known-open, pre-existing and orthogonal: global-scope
carrier loads miscompile through the x9 address peephole; RISC-V
needs the f128_in_gp_pairs classification arm.
